{"tool_name":"fastseg","contents":["bioconda","oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"bioconda":{"name":"bioconductor-fastseg","version":"1.56.0","home":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/html/fastseg.html","license":"LGPL (>= 2.0)","summary":"fastseg - a fast segmentation algorithm","identifiers":["biotools:fastseg"],"description":"fastseg implements a very fast and efficient segmentation algorithm. It has similar functionality as DNACopy (Olshen and Venkatraman 2004), but is considerably faster and more flexible. fastseg can segment data from DNA microarrays and data from next generation sequencing for example to detect copy number segments. Further it can segment data from RNA microarrays like tiling arrays to identify transcripts. Most generally, it can segment data given as a matrix or as a vector. Various data formats can be used as input to fastseg like expression set objects for microarrays or GRanges for sequencing data. The segmentation criterion of fastseg is based on a statistical test in a Bayesian framework, namely the cyber t-test (Baldi 2001). The speed-up arises from the facts, that sampling is not necessary in for fastseg and that a dynamic programming approach is used for calculation of the segments' first and higher order moments.","license_file":"/lib/R/share/licenses/LGPL-3","build":{"number":0,"rpaths":["lib/R/lib/","lib/"],"run_exports":""},"requirements":{"build":[null,null,"make"],"host":["bioconductor-biobase >=2.70.0,<2.71.0","bioconductor-biocgenerics >=0.56.0,<0.57.0","bioconductor-genomicranges >=1.62.0,<1.63.0","bioconductor-iranges >=2.44.0,<2.45.0","bioconductor-s4vectors >=0.48.0,<0.49.0","r-base","libblas","liblapack","zlib","liblzma-devel"],"run":["bioconductor-biobase >=2.70.0,<2.71.0","bioconductor-biocgenerics >=0.56.0,<0.57.0","bioconductor-genomicranges >=1.62.0,<1.63.0","bioconductor-iranges >=2.44.0,<2.45.0","bioconductor-s4vectors >=0.48.0,<0.49.0","r-base"]},"source_url":["https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/src/contrib/fastseg_1.56.0.tar.gz","https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/src/contrib/Archive/fastseg/fastseg_1.56.0.tar.gz","https://bioarchive.galaxyproject.org/fastseg_1.56.0.tar.gz","https://depot.galaxyproject.org/software/bioconductor-fastseg/bioconductor-fastseg_1.56.0_src_all.tar.gz"],"test":{"commands":["$R -e \"library('fastseg')\""]},"parent_recipe":{"name":"bioconductor-fastseg","path":"recipes/bioconductor-fastseg","version":"1.26.0"}},"biotools":{"id":"fastseg","home":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/fastseg","license":"LGPL-2.0-or-later","summary":"fastseg implements a very fast and efficient segmentation algorithm. It has similar functionality as DNACopy (Olshen and Venkatraman 2004), but is considerably faster and more flexible. fastseg can segment data from DNA microarrays and data from next generation sequencing for example to detect copy number segments. Further it can segment data from RNA microarrays like tiling arrays to identify transcripts. Most generally, it can segment data given as a matrix or as a vector. Various data formats can be used as input to fastseg like expression set objects for microarrays or GRanges for sequencing data. The segmentation criterion of fastseg is based on a statistical test in a Bayesian framework, namely the cyber t-test (Baldi 2001). The speed-up arises from the facts, that sampling is not necessary in for fastseg and that a dynamic programming approach is used for calculation of the segments' first and higher order moments.","addition_date":"2017-01-17T15:00:14Z","last_update_date":"2026-08-31T21:26:37.762043Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Library"],"version":["1.58.0"],"collections":["BioConductor"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:fastseg","credit":[{"name":"Alexander Blume","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Maintainer"]},{"name":"Guenter Klambauer","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"]},{"name":"Sonali Kumari","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/fastseg"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2403"}]}],"name":"fastseg","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"bioconductor_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gks003"}],"topic":[{"term":"Microarray experiment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3518"},{"term":"Sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0080"},{"term":"Sequencing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3168"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"fastseg","home":"https://bio.tools/fastseg","license":"LGPL-2.0-or-later","version":"1.58.0","summary":"cn.mops (Copy Number estimation by a Mixture Of PoissonS) is a data processing pipeline for copy number variations and aberrations (CNVs and CNAs) from next generation sequencing (NGS) data. The package supplies functions to convert BAM files into read count matrices or genomic ranges objects, which are the input objects for cn.mops. cn.mops models the depths of coverage across samples at each genomic position. Therefore, it does not suffer from read count biases along chromosomes. Using a Bayesian approach, cn.mops decomposes read variations across samples into integer copy numbers and noise by its mixture components and Poisson distributions, respectively. cn.mops guarantees a low FDR because wrong detections are indicated by high noise and filtered out. cn.mops is very fast and written in C++.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:fastseg:1.20.0/cmd/bioconductor.org","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":true}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"manual":true},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_coun